WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.113
Addition, subtraction, and multiplication
get most of the attention,

2
00:00:04.193 --> 00:00:07.505
but division, remainder, and shifts also
need defensive review.

3
00:00:07.585 --> 00:00:11.498
These operations often appear in alignment
code, bucket selection, paging,

4
00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:16.034
hashing support, bit masks, record layout,
size rounding, and capacity calculations.

5
00:00:16.114 --> 00:00:18.583
Division and remainder require divisor
checks.

6
00:00:18.663 --> 00:00:21.553
Dividing by zero is not a recoverable
arithmetic result.

7
00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:24.945
If a divisor comes from configuration,
input, metadata, a count,

8
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.036
or an earlier calculation, verify it
before the operation.

9
00:00:28.116 --> 00:00:32.331
The check should be close enough to the
operation that later changes do not separate

10
00:00:32.411 --> 00:00:33.795
the guarantee from the use.

11
00:00:33.875 --> 00:00:37.970
Signed minimum divided by negative one is
a special overflow edge case for common

12
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.796
two's-complement-style ranges.

13
00:00:39.876 --> 00:00:44.272
Defensive code that performs signed
division near type boundaries should account for

14
00:00:44.352 --> 00:00:45.737
this case when it can arise.

15
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:49.068
Edge values should be included in tests,
not left to assumption.

16
00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:53.604
Remainder results should not be assumed
positive without understanding operand signs

17
00:00:53.684 --> 00:00:54.888
and language behavior.

18
00:00:54.968 --> 00:00:57.678
Code that uses a remainder as an index,
bucket number,

19
00:00:57.758 --> 00:01:01.913
or offset should make sure the resulting
value is within the intended nonnegative

20
00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:02.354
range.

21
00:01:02.434 --> 00:01:06.167
When in doubt, validate the value before
it reaches an indexing decision.

22
00:01:06.247 --> 00:01:10.101
Shift counts must be nonnegative and less
than the width of the promoted left

23
00:01:10.181 --> 00:01:10.662
operand.

24
00:01:10.742 --> 00:01:15.018
Left shifts can overflow or produce
unintended results depending on signedness and

25
00:01:15.098 --> 00:01:15.459
value.

26
00:01:15.539 --> 00:01:19.453
Right shifts of signed values also deserve
care when code relies on a specific

27
00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:21.520
representation or rounding behavior.

28
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.828
Bit masks, flags, alignment calculations,

29
00:01:23.908 --> 00:01:27.822
and power-of-two calculations should
document their numeric assumptions.

30
00:01:27.902 --> 00:01:31.153
Name the width, signedness, allowed range,
and failure policy.

31
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:35.388
A small helper with a reviewed contract is
often easier to maintain than scattered

32
00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:38.900
expressions that rely on everyone
remembering the same edge cases.
